WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.330
Film Courage: What are the basics of getting
a great shot with any camera?

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.650
From an iPhone to a DSLR?

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:11.680
Andy Rydzewski, Cinematographer: I suppose
it’s a little bit like your previous question

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:19.329
about a beautiful shot like what makes a great
shot because it’s so reliant on context

00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:30.470
so any shot is where is it going to fit into
the montage of images so its place is really

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:39.130
more what I think about but on a technical
level there are certain things that I will

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:44.200
always be careful of.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.300
Usually you want to see (unless there is a
reason you don’t) but seeing an actor’s

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.910
eyes is important or at least one eye.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.559
We as humans we connect to eyes.

00:00:54.559 --> 00:01:02.280
If you take googly eyes and you put them on
a fork suddenly you can bond with that fork

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.390
like it suddenly has human characteristics.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.550
I think eyes are a big thing to I don’t
think about it consciously but I know looking

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:17.310
at a shot that I am shooting figuring out
where the eyes are and can you see them and

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:23.350
are there other things in the frame that are
drawing your eye as opposed to an actor’s

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.350
face?

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:34.740
That’s a big part of it so I think maybe
the way I’d put it is I want to know in

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.220
a shot where do I want the audience to look?

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:44.630
How do I want them to feel when they look
there and if you have a big wide shot and

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:52.290
there is an actor somewhere but there are
other elements in the frame like a big turquoise

00:01:52.290 --> 00:02:00.840
teddy bear or a big shiny something or other
and maybe that helps the scene?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.640
Maybe that helps the shot but I need to know
what the shot is intended to do, the ideal

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.940
is and then I can work from there.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:19.330
Often you end up with a frame (you start with
a frame rather) where there is too much pulling

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:25.280
the eye and I know this shot you need to go
to that actor right away so that means we

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.870
need to knock down this wall.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:32.890
This wall is too bright so let’s make sure
we knock this light down so it’s not hitting

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.020
this wall.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.970
Sometimes there is something like an element
of costume that like “Whoah!”

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.280
In this shot these red boots are too much.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.470
Maybe we frame them out?

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:49.320
But it becomes I guess that’s really what
it is for me is it becomes about what is the

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.660
intention and where do you want people to
look?

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:58.390
And then you start almost chiseling, it’s
a sculpture and you’re trying to get rid

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:03.870
of the pieces that don’t need to be there
so that people are drawn to the piece be it

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:09.950
through composition or lighting or color or
size in frame.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.700
But I think that would be how I would approach
it.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:21.570
And then you know in terms of different cameras
have different latitudes so like an Arri Alexa

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.709
has 15-ish stops of latitude so you could
have your dark to your light.

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:34.290
An iPhone is significantly less and if something
if there is a white spot that is blown out,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:43.441
it’s just no information and it can be ugly
and it can pull your eye because it’s so

00:03:43.441 --> 00:03:44.930
bright and bare.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:52.060
So there are technical limitations to different
sensors and that can also be a part of it

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.690
is something that is less artistically driven
and more technical and well this camera’s

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:03.610
sensor is going to break unless we knock down
this light and shoot it all within a better

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.610
latitude.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.849
But again the intention is the same because
you don’t want them looking at the wrong

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:13.660
thing and having their mind drift to something
else.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.289
You are sort of trying to be intentional about
everything within a frame.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:18.289
Question For The Viewers: When it comes to
getting great shots, what do you struggle

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:18.292
with the most?

